WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:14.160
TARNISHED REPUTATION:&nbsp;

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:26.400
Volkswagen is one of the largest and most trusted&nbsp;
automotive brands in the entire world. Not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:32.640
do they own the brand Volkswagen, but they’ve&nbsp;
also owned Audi, Porsche, Lamborghini, Bentley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:39.540
and even Bugatti at on point. But, despite their&nbsp;
size, Volkswagen doesn’t exactly have the best of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:45.660
reputations especially in recent years. It seems&nbsp;
that their evergrowing size has simply allowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:51.780
for bureaucracy and politics to seep in, and when&nbsp;
these attributes are present, it’s usually just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:58.140
matter of time until the company starts putting&nbsp;
money over morals and ethics. This is exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:03.660
what happened in the mid 2010s when regulators&nbsp;
discovered that Volkswagen was cheating emissions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:09.960
tests. And this wasn’t some sort of isolated&nbsp;
incidence either. Volkswagen had been cheating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:17.100
on emissions tests for 6 years between 2009&nbsp;
and 2015, and their antics had affected half a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:22.800
million cars in the US and another 10.5 million&nbsp;
cars outside the US. After this came to light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:30.000
Volkswagen was immediately fined $14.7 billion and&nbsp;
this was just the first of several settlements,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.460
lawsuits, and criminal investigations. By the end&nbsp;
of the saga, the emissions scandal cost Volkswagen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:42.780
a total of 31.3 billion euros or a little under&nbsp;
$35 billion. Usually, companies have a history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:48.900
of getting away with relatively cheap fines, but&nbsp;
this was clearly not one of those scenarios. Not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:55.380
to mention, the financial loss wasn’t even their&nbsp;
biggest concern. Nowadays people are acutely aware&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.380 --> 00:02:00.480
of the importance of clean energy, minimizing&nbsp;
pollution, and protecting the earth. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:05.220
millions of people around the world think that&nbsp;
it’s so important that they’re willing to shell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:10.680
out substantially more money to buy an EV over an&nbsp;
gasoline vehicle. So, with this type of history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:16.140
there’s no question that Volkswagen alienated&nbsp;
a significant portion of the market. With these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:19.860
types of consequences, you would think&nbsp;
that companies would think twice before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:25.440
engaging in such activities. But, given the&nbsp;
consistency of scandals amongst corporate giants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:31.320
this is clearly not to case. So, here’s&nbsp;
how Volkswagen got caught up in arguably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:36.240
the largest automotive scandal ever and&nbsp;
stained their reputation permanently.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:40.080
A HISTORY OF SCANDALS:&nbsp;

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:44.580
While Volkswagen didn’t make international&nbsp;
headlines until the emissions scandal, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:50.460
company has very much had a history of scandals,&nbsp;
but they weren’t always the culprit. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:55.260
at one point in time, Volkswagen was actually&nbsp;
on the other side of the fraud as a victim.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.260 --> 00:03:02.460
Back in March of 1987, Volkswagen would announce&nbsp;
that they had lost as much as $258 million due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:07.380
to fraudulent foreign exchange deals. As an&nbsp;
international conglomerate, Volkswagen often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:12.300
deals with dozens of currencies, and it’s their&nbsp;
responsibility to proactively convert between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:16.440
these currencies and hedge their reserves&nbsp;
to ensure that they don’t lose money due to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:22.440
fluctuations in currency value. In 1985, they had&nbsp;
made one such routine hedge against the US dollar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:27.180
but it turns out that the person selling them&nbsp;
the hedge had forged the documents. Fortunately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:32.280
for Volkswagen, the FBI would eventually&nbsp;
arrest the suspect several months later. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:37.680
unfortuantley for everyone else, this was the last&nbsp;
time that Volkswagen was on the victim side of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:43.080
scandal. Volkswagen didn’t just instantly jump to&nbsp;
international schemes though. As with most things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:49.200
Volkswagen’s descent into the darkness was much&nbsp;
more gradual and almost indiscernible. It started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:54.060
off with some misbehaving executives who were&nbsp;
more interested in personal gain. Take Klaus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:59.580
Volkert for example. Klaus was the representative&nbsp;
of Volkswagen’s 300,000 workers since the early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:05.940
1990s. From the very beginning, workers weren’t&nbsp;
exactly a fan of Klaus. They felt that Klaus was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:10.260
very much on the side of the leadership than&nbsp;
the side of the laborers. But, in reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:15.660
it turns out that the only team Klaus was really&nbsp;
on was his own. During his time at the top,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:20.580
Klaus would abuse his power to pay out $3&nbsp;
million worth of illegal bonuses which included&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:26.460
$590,000 to a Brazilian lover. This earned&nbsp;
Klaus convictions on 48 counts of inciting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:31.980
fraud along with 2 years and 9 months in jail.&nbsp;
But, Klaus was just one of many corrupt leaders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:37.860
at Volkswagen. Another corrupt leader was Peter&nbsp;
Hartz. Unlike Klaus though, Peter actually had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:43.080
ambitions beyond just helping himself. He of&nbsp;
course did plenty of that too though. He was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:48.300
notorious for using company funds to pay for&nbsp;
escorts and certain types of parties. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:53.460
more than that, Peter had a history of trying to&nbsp;
bribe people. He bribed Volkswagen suppliers to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:59.040
make it seem like he had won Volkwagen insanely&nbsp;
lucrative deals. He also bribed the Union to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:04.440
it seem like he had more worker support than he&nbsp;
really did. The only place all this led him though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:09.840
was facing 44 counts of breach of trust along&nbsp;
with a 2 year prison sentence. And these were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:14.820
just the most notable cases. Some other Volkswagen&nbsp;
representatives would trick an Indian state into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:20.100
paying them a bunch of money for a factory that&nbsp;
was never built. As you can see, Volkswagen was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:25.140
filled with these crony leaders by the late 2000s,&nbsp;
but none of these guys were truly the dangerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:31.860
ones. The truly dangerous ones don’t care about&nbsp;
winning money, they care about winning reputation.

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:35.760
EMISSIONS SCANDAL:&nbsp;

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:40.020
Regulators were by no means looking for&nbsp;
an emissions scandal when they started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:45.360
testing Volkswagen cars. They were actually&nbsp;
just conducting a boring old study that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:50.220
trying to identify the emissions differences&nbsp;
between European cars and American cars. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:55.560
to accomplish this, they hired five scientists&nbsp;
from West Virginia university. As scientists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.560 --> 00:06:00.660
these five set out to eliminate as many variables&nbsp;
as possible except for the ones that were being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:06.000
studied. One of the variables that they eliminated&nbsp;
was the emissions testing system. Instead of using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.740
systems provided by the manufactuer, they used&nbsp;
a Japanese emission testing system for all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:16.500
vehicles and this is when they noticed something&nbsp;
alarming. Volkswagen cars weren’t just emitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:21.180
more pollutants than they claimed, but they were&nbsp;
emitting so much that they were breaking laws.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:26.940
Take the Volkswagen Jetta for example. According&nbsp;
to previous tests, the Jetta was only outputting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.940 --> 00:06:33.900
0.022 g of nitrogen oxides for every kilometer&nbsp;
of driving. This was well below the EPA limit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:40.740
0.043 g/km. It was basically half the limit.&nbsp;
But, West Virginia found that the Jetta was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:47.040
actually emitting anywhere between 0.61 to 1.5 g&nbsp;
of nitrogen oxides for evey kilometer of driving.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:52.560
Even their lowest reading was 14 times the&nbsp;
allowable limit while the highest reading was 34&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:58.080
time the allowable limit, and a very similar story&nbsp;
could be seen with a bunch of other Volkswagen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:04.320
cars as well. When the researchers published their&nbsp;
findings, the entire world was shocked, but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:10.440
were also puzzled. How was Volkswagen able to pass&nbsp;
millions emissions tests for years if they had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:15.480
disgustingly high emissions? They couldn’t just&nbsp;
pay off a couple of testers or give them special&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:21.600
vehicles because they’re not the only ones who are&nbsp;
conducting exams. A substantial number states in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:27.000
the US requires that car owners get their vehicle&nbsp;
emissions certified on a regular basis. So, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:32.460
wasn’t just a matter of faking tests a handful of&nbsp;
times. This was a matter of consistently faking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:39.120
hundreds of thousands if not millions of exams.&nbsp;
So, how did Volkswagen manage to pull this off for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:45.600
years? Well, it turns out that clever engineering&nbsp;
can easily bypass exams conducted by poorly funded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:50.760
regulatory bodies. What Volkswagen had done&nbsp;
was install special emissions software onto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:55.920
11 million cars around the world. This software&nbsp;
was capable of detecting when routine emissions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:59.520
exams were being conducted and it would&nbsp;
change a whole bunch of variables to reduce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:05.040
emissions. It changed things like fuel pressure,&nbsp;
injection timing, exhaust gas recirculation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:11.040
and the amount of urea sprayed into the exhaust.&nbsp;
While this was extremely clever, all you really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:15.120
had to do to find the real emissions was use an&nbsp;
external testing device that wouldn’t set off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:21.060
Volkswagen’s systems. But somehow, regulatory&nbsp;
bodies had failed to do this for 6 years.

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:25.140
THE FALLOUT:&nbsp;

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:31.020
One of the biggest questions that you might have&nbsp;
is why didn’t Volkswagen just set their cars to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.020 --> 00:08:36.540
always be in this mode. They were clearly able&nbsp;
to pass emissions exams with flying colors using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:42.720
this software, so why not use it all the time?&nbsp;
Well, the problem was that this would severely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:47.520
limit the power and speed of their vehicles.&nbsp;
Their cars wouldn’t be able to hit the promised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:53.460
0 to 60 numbers or horsepower and torque numbers.&nbsp;
If you’re not into cars, this probably seems like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:58.080
a non issue, but Volkswagen felt that reducing&nbsp;
these numbers would go against their brand image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:03.300
and appeal. Here’s the thing, the people who are&nbsp;
buying German cars aren’t looking for something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:09.420
economical or environmental. If they were, they&nbsp;
would just buy a Japanese car. Rather, people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:16.200
buying German cars buy them for three reasons:&nbsp;
luxury, social status, and power. This is why Audi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:20.580
owners are willing to deal with the more expensive&nbsp;
maintenance bills and lower reliability. If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:24.720
substantially reduce the power of these vehicles,&nbsp;
however, much fewer people would be willing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:31.020
deal with the associated headaches, and many of&nbsp;
them would likely buy a Mercedes or BMW instead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:36.660
With that being said though, it probably would’ve&nbsp;
been a better idea for Volkswagen to just spend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.660 --> 00:09:41.820
more time figuring out how to minimize emissions&nbsp;
while maximizing power instead of faking exams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:47.700
but that woud be too logical. Anyway, Volkswagen&nbsp;
would eventually admit to installing this secret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:53.340
software and they would agree to buyback cars from&nbsp;
owners at a pretty generous price. Owners not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:58.380
received the pre scandal trade in value of their&nbsp;
cars, but they also received $5 to $10,000 in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.380 --> 00:10:03.900
additional compensation. Volkwagen also agreed to&nbsp;
spend $4.7 billion on programs looking to offset&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:09.540
emissions and boost clean vehicle projects. So,&nbsp;
financially speaking, the public actually came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:15.360
out pretty well, but the same cannot be said&nbsp;
about pollution and health concerns. Volkswagen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:20.820
literally had over 10 million cars on the road for&nbsp;
years emitting up to 40 times the legal amount.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:26.760
And these limits aren’t in place just for fun.&nbsp;
Extremely intelligent scientists have determined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:31.200
that vehicles emitting more than a certain amount&nbsp;
are more of a detriment to society than a benefit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:37.560
And we’re not just talking about climate change&nbsp;
or melting polar caps, we’re talking about lung&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:43.860
cancer and lots of it. In the European Union for&nbsp;
example, 20% of urban populations are subject&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:50.220
to hazardous nitrogen dioxide concentrations. And&nbsp;
40% of all nitrogen oxide emissions in London come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:57.420
from diesel road traffic. Every year, 3000 people&nbsp;
die due to air pollution in London alone. So, who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.420 --> 00:11:02.820
knows how many people Volkswagen’s high emissions&nbsp;
truly affected and potentially even killed.

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:04.380
A NEW LEAF:&nbsp;

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:12.120
The worst part about all of this is that&nbsp;
Volkswagen wasn’t the only one that was guilty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:17.280
of faking exams. After the news broke about&nbsp;
Volkswagen, regulators started looking into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:22.440
all cars more closely, and they quickly realized&nbsp;
that a lot of cars had much better readings in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:28.080
exams than on the road. On the brightside, this&nbsp;
has prompted regulators to tighten their testing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:34.860
procedures, but the real solution is of course&nbsp;
EVs. And it looks like Volkswagen is working very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:39.840
hard to redeem themselves and earn back public&nbsp;
trust. While Volkswagen had some doubts about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:44.700
pivoting to EVs in the early days, more recently,&nbsp;
they’ve basically completely committed to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:50.100
switch. More than half of all of Volkswagen’s&nbsp;
budget over the next 5 years is going towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.100 --> 00:11:56.400
EVs which works out to $100 billion. They’re&nbsp;
pouring insane amounts into battery research,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.400 --> 00:12:01.500
building new factories, and most importantly&nbsp;
scaling up production. They’re hoping that by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:07.440
2026, a quarter of all the vehicles they sell will&nbsp;
be electric. And given that Volkswagen regularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:12.420
sells 10 million cars per year, that works&nbsp;
out to well above 2 million EVs. At this rate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.420 --> 00:12:18.900
some experts say that Volkswagen may even overtake&nbsp;
Tesla in terms of EV deliveries, but we’ll just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:23.040
have to wait and see. Do you think Volkswagen&nbsp;
can redeem themselves by pushing forward the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:28.860
EV revolution? Comment that down below. Also,&nbsp;
drop a like if you’re glad more companies are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.860 --> 00:12:33.480
embracing EVs. And of course, consider checking&nbsp;
